p.src:
A cautious reconstruction of the lecture’s compounding logic is $\begin{aligned}
K_{t+1} &= K_t + L_t,\\
Y_{t+k} &= f\!\left(\sum_{i\le t} L_i\right),\end{aligned}$ where $K_t$ is usable capability, $L_t$ is one learning input, and $Y_{t+k}$ is later business output. The function $f$ should not be overread. It simply preserves the spoken claim that later revenue often comes from the accumulation of many earlier learning inputs rather than from one magical course.

